puzzle -- Compute puzzles with given boundaries
***********************************************

  * Usage: 
        puzzle (a,b)
        puzzle (a,b,c)
  * Inputs:
      * a, a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, 
      * b, a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, 
      * c, a *note string: (Macaulay2Doc)String,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Ktheory => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value
        false
      * Equivariant => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value true
      * Generic => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value
        true
      * Ktheory' => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, has an effect only if Generic => false
      * Labels => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value
        true
      * Paths => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value
        false
      * Steps => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value null
      * Separation (missing documentation) => ..., default value null, 

Description
===========

This function produces a list of puzzles with boundaries given by strings a
(northwest side), b (northeast side), c (bottom side). The default
Generic=>true produces "generic" puzzles corresponding to the multiplication of
motivic Segre classes *note sClass: segreClass,, whereas Generic=>false
produces "classic" puzzles corresponding to the multiplication of Schubert
classes *note schubertClass: schubertClass, (in the latter case, Ktheory'=>true
produces puzzles for dual Schubert classes).

The boundaries are encoded as strings of digits from "0" to "d", where d is the
number of steps of the flag variety; alternatively, one can use lists of
integers or strings (the latter allows for the possibility of having arbitrary
puzzle labels on the boundary, such as "2(10)", even though this will not occur
in normal puzzle computations). The special symbol "#" stands for any single
digit, whereas "*" stands for any puzzle label.

Labels and Paths are drawing options which only affect HTML and TeX output of
puzzles.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : puzzle ("0101","1001",Equivariant=>false)                               |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = {                   /\                   ,                              |
